 How do we divide polynomials?  We can use long division, but first we have to know the "quotient" $q(x)$ and "remainder" $r(x)$ such that
\[
\frac{x^3-3x+5}{x+2} = q(x) + \frac{r(x)}{x+2}.
\]If $x = -2,$ then
\begin{align*}
\frac{(-2)^3 - 3(-2) + 5}{-2 + 2} &= q(-2) + \frac{r(-2)}{-2 + 2} \\
&= q(-2) + \frac{r(-2)}{0}.
\end{align*}Thus, $r(-2) = -9.$  Continuing with long division, we get
\[
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^2} & -5x & + 13 \\ \cline{2-5}
x+2 & x^3 & -3x & +5  &   \\ \cline{2-5}
 & x^3 & +2x^2 &   &   \\ \cline{2-3}
 & & -2x^2 & -3x &   \\ \cline{3-4}
 & & -2x^2 & -4x &   \\ \cline{4-5}
 & & & x & +5  \\ \cline{5-5}
 & & & x & +2   \\ \cline{5-5}
  & & & & 3
\end{array}
\]Our quotient is $x^2 - 5x + 13,$ and our remainder is $\boxed{3}.$ </think> <answer> 3 </answer>

